{"name":"Flower Stem","hex":"#b5d5b0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flower stem","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The stem of a flower."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower_stem"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plant stem","blocks":[{"text":"A stem is one of two main structural axes of a vascular plant, the other being the root. It supports leaves, flowers and fruits, transports water and dissolved substances between the roots and the shoots in the xylem and phloem, engages in photosynthesis, stores nutrients, and produces new living tissue. The stem can also be called the culm, halm, haulm, stalk, or thyrsus. The woody stem of a tree is known as a trunk."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/Plant_nodes_c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant_stem","wikidata":"Q134267"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower","wikidata":"Q506"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Polygonum_amphibium_%284973671699%29.jpg/500px-Polygonum_amphibium_%284973671699%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Polygonum_amphibium_%284973671699%29.jpg/1280px-Polygonum_amphibium_%284973671699%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Polygonum_amphibium_%284973671699%29.jpg/500px-Polygonum_amphibium_%284973671699%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This above-ground stem of Polygonum has lost its leaves, but is producing adventitious roots from the nodes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolygonum_amphibium_(4973671699).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Illustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png/500px-Illustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Illustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png/1280px-Illustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Illustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png/500px-Illustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration and diagram of some types of stems","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_of_various_types_of_plant_stems.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Senecio_angulatus_stems.jpg/500px-Senecio_angulatus_stems.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Senecio_angulatus_stems.jpg/1280px-Senecio_angulatus_stems.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25